Patch 09c: transform partition stationarity, next step along the margin curve.
Supersedes 09b (and 09) -- apply INSTEAD of either, never on top.

09b passed on both classes at Speed 4 and improved on 09 in every dimension:

    MARGIN 4 (patch 09)    A1 +2.60% / +0.11%  ratio 23.6
                           A2 +2.27% / +0.09%  ratio 25.2
    MARGIN 2 (patch 09b)   A1 +4.01% / +0.15%  ratio 26.7  PASS
                           A2 +3.57% / +0.07%  ratio 51.0  PASS

Two facts from that comparison drive this patch. Speed rose ~1.55x on both
classes while BD-rate rose only 1.36x on A1 and *fell* on A2, so loosening was
ratio-positive, not merely ratio-neutral. And A2's BD-rate of 0.07% is small
enough to be indistinguishable from measurement noise, so its ratio of 51 is
speed divided by noise and should not be read as 155% of genuine headroom.

The single change here is TX_PART_STATIONARITY_MARGIN 2 -> 1, loosening the
stationarity test from "strongest strip within 1.50x of mean" to "within
2.00x".

What this is and is not: two points trending favourably do not establish that
the third does. A pruning threshold must decay as it loosens -- eventually
every block is judged stationary and the quality cost runs away. This patch
tests where that decay begins. A1 is the binding class, holding 33% headroom
above the bar of 20 against A2's much larger margin, so A1 is where it will
fail first if it fails.

If it does, no patch edit is needed to recover: MARGIN 2 is measured and
known-good, so build with -DTX_PART_STATIONARITY_MARGIN=2 and you are back to
the 09b operating point exactly. Running 09c and 09b as two arms of the same
round would bracket the curve and cost one extra arm.

The lazy-evaluation change from 09b is unchanged and carried forward: the
stationarity measurement stays deferred until after the cheap eligibility
checks, and is skipped entirely when the only surviving candidate is
TX_PARTITION_NONE.

